    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The guide looks okay so far, but I'm worried about the \"threat actors\" part. We've had some issues with phishing attempts lately. How much better are these hardware keys at stopping those specific types of attacks compared to the SMS codes we're using now?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to be concerned about the phishing threats, and it is a good thing you are thinking about the \"threat actors\" before the full rollout. Hardware security keys like YubiKeys are significantly better at stopping phishing attacks compared to SMS codes because they rely on the FIDO2 standard, which uses public-key cryptography that is mathematically impossible to intercept or spoof via a fake website. Unlike SMS codes, which can be intercepted via SIM swapping or social engineering, a YubiKey requires physical possession and a specific cryptographic handshake that a phishing site cannot replicate.\n\nHowever, even with these superior defenses, you must remember that no security measure is 100% bulletproof, and the old SMS method was just as vulnerable to social engineering as a human could be tricked into handing over a key. To truly minimize the risk, you should consider implementing a \"phishing-resistant\" policy where users are trained to recognize that if a link looks suspicious, they should not click it, regardless of whether they have a YubiKey or not.\n\nIn terms of deployment, you might want to add a step in your guide where you simulate a phishing attack during the pilot phase to ensure your team's new keys are being used correctly and",
